WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.040
The South China Sea matters for a number of
reasons. Something like five trillion

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.080
dollars of trade pass through the sea

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.680
each and every year. It's an important
route by which Middle Eastern energy

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.430
supplies make their way to the major
Asian economies of China, Taiwan, Japan,

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:24.520
and South Korea. Put simply, it links
major economies together worldwide.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.120
But it's become a key focus of U.S. foreign
policy in recent years due to a number

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:32.050
of maritime territorial disputes that
involve a number of governments in the

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.489
region, including those of China, Taiwan,
the Philippines, Brunei, Malaysia, and

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:39.730
Vietnam. Sometimes Indonesia is thrown
into the mix, as well.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.600
Of particular concern to the United
States is the expansive nature of

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.539
China's claims, and the way it's going
about asserting them.

00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:50.019
It has snatched territory from other
countries. It's denied foreign fishermen

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:52.210
access to their traditional fishing
grounds.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.589
It's made it much more dangerous for the
United States' military assets to operate

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:00.039
in the region, and it's built artificial
islands out of whole cloth.

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:03.819
Put simply, it's challenging the freedom
of navigation that all countries,

00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:06.670
including China, have benefited from for
decades.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.240
So what are your thoughts on the South
China Sea?

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.510
Let us know in the comments, and also let
us know what other topics you want

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:17.500
AEI scholars to cover in 60 seconds.

